@startuml
title Decorator - Toast (Solution) - Class Diagram

skinparam classAttributeIconSize 0
skinparam nodesep 45
skinparam ranksep 55

interface Toastable <<Component>> {
  +calculatePrice(): int
  +getToppings(): List<Topping>
}

class ToastBread <<ConcreteComponent>> {
  -name: String
  -price: int
  +calculatePrice(): int
  +getToppings(): List<Topping>
}

abstract class Topping <<Decorator>> {
  #component: Toastable
  -name: String
  -price: int
  +calculatePrice(): int
  +getToppings(): List<Topping>
}

class Cheese <<ConcreteDecorator>>
class Sausage <<ConcreteDecorator>>
class Tomato <<ConcreteDecorator>>
class Ketchup <<ConcreteDecorator>>
class Salad <<ConcreteDecorator>>

class Promotion <<ConcreteDecorator>> {
  -component: Toastable
  -percentageOff: int
  +calculatePrice(): int
  +getToppings(): List<Topping>
}

Toastable <|.. ToastBread
Toastable <|.. Topping
Toastable <|.. Promotion
Topping o--> "1" Toastable : component
Promotion o--> "1" Toastable : component

Topping <|-- Cheese
Topping <|-- Sausage
Topping <|-- Tomato
Topping <|-- Ketchup
Topping <|-- Salad

note right of Topping
  **Both at once**, and that is the pattern:

  Topping **implements** Toastable,
  so a topped toast goes wherever
  a plain one goes.

  Topping **holds** a Toastable,
  so it forwards the question down
  and adds its own answer.

  Being both is what lets toppings
  stack without limit.
end note

note bottom of Promotion
  A decorator that is **not** a topping.

  It multiplies instead of adding, and
  implements Toastable directly, so the
  compiler can prove it never appears
  in getToppings().

  Decorators need no common base class
  with each other, only a common type
  with what they wrap.
end note

note as N1
  **Five classes, not thirty-one.**

  Adding a topping to the menu is one new
  class. Adding one to an order is one new
  object, at run time, after the customer
  has changed their mind.
end note

@enduml
